// **A computed file-scope `#insert` followed by constants that a body reads** (ADR-0188 §1).
//
// # Why this file exists, and why it did not before
//
// A constant's value is keyed by `ItemId`. `file_consts` evaluates the **unexpanded** tree, and a
// computed `#insert` then adds an item, so every `ItemId` after the splice moves — and the lookup
// missed. The symptom was E0245, *"a file-level item has no value until jr-vm"*, on a body reading a
// constant declared plainly as `A :: 11;`.
//
// ADR-0184 §2 wrote that hazard down, and the code beside it already cleared and re-recorded the
// *other* map keyed the same way. This one was missed. So the defect was not a surprise about how
// expansion works — it was one of two maps fixed.
//
// **Nothing could have caught it**, which is the point of this file: it needs a computed insert *and*
// constants after it *and* a body that reads one, and no corpus program combined the three. ADR-0184's
// own coverage (`valid/136`) uses a **literal** insert, which expands before const-eval and so shifts
// nothing that matters.
//
// # What is pinned
//
//   * four constants declared **after** the splice, each read by a body — the case that failed;
//   * a constant declared **before** the splice, which never shifted and must still work;
//   * a *procedure* declared after the splice and called, since a proc is reached by `ProcId` rather
//     than by the const map and must be unaffected;
//   * the generated declaration itself being **used**, so the insert is load-bearing rather than
//     decorative — a splice nothing consumes could be silently dropped and this file would still pass.
//
// Exit code 31 = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16.
#import "Basic";

// Declared before the splice: its `ItemId` cannot move, so it is the control.
BEFORE :: 7;

// The generated declaration. A `#system_library` is what a computed operand may generate (ADR-0184 §4),
// and `"c"` is a name that links on every target this project builds for.
generated :: () -> string {
    return "libc :: #system_library \"c\";";
}

#insert #run generated();

// Every one of these has an `ItemId` **after** the splice. Before the fix, a body reading any of them
// was refused.
A :: 11;
B :: 22;
C :: 33;
D :: 44;

// A procedure after the splice too, called below. Reached by `ProcId`, so it should never have been
// affected — asserted rather than assumed, because "should never" is how the other map got missed.
after_splice :: (n: s64) -> s64 {
    return n * 2;
}

// Uses the generated library declaration, so the splice is load-bearing. `abs` is in libc everywhere.
c_abs :: (n: s32) -> s32 #foreign libc "abs";

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    if BEFORE == 7 {
        n = n + 1;
    }
    if A == 11 && B == 22 {
        n = n + 2;
    }
    if C == 33 && D == 44 {
        n = n + 4;
    }
    if after_splice(21) == 42 {
        n = n + 8;
    }
    if c_abs(-5) == 5 {
        n = n + 16;
    }

    exit(n);
}
